=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_3
diagnosis_text:
During this morning's firewall change window, an erroneous iptables rule was
inserted into the FORWARD chain on router_core_3:

  FORWARD rule 1: DROP  protocol all  source 0.0.0.0/0  destination 10.200.0.0/24

router_core_3 is the sole L3 gateway between the two user pods and the server
farm: pod traffic flows host → switch_dist → router_core_1/2 → router_core_3
→ switch_server_access → servers. Any forwarded packet whose destination falls
within 10.200.0.0/24 is silently dropped at the kernel netfilter FORWARD hook
on router_core_3 before it can be handed off to eth2. The rule is protocol-
and source-agnostic, so ICMP, UDP (DNS), and TCP (HTTP/HTTPS) are all affected
equally — hence DNS timeouts and portal failures for both pods simultaneously.

Pod-to-pod traffic is unaffected because those packets are destined for
10.1.1.0/24 or 10.2.1.0/24, not 10.200.0.0/24. OSPF adjacencies and routing
tables are fully healthy on all routers; switch_server_access has correct
back-routes to both pod subnets and carries no firewall rules of its own.
Intra-server-farm traffic never transits router_core_3's FORWARD chain so it
works normally. The rule counter (12 packets / 1008 bytes) confirms it is
actively dropping live traffic.

Root cause: a blanket iptables FORWARD DROP for 10.200.0.0/24 was mistakenly
applied to router_core_3 during the firewall change window.

Corrective fix: on router_core_3, run:
  iptables -D FORWARD -d 10.200.0.0/24 -j DROP
This removes rule 1 from the FORWARD chain and immediately restores pod-to-
server connectivity without any routing or OSPF changes needed.
=== END ===
```